## 2026-07-23 - US-005

- Implemented drag-to-move and drag-to-resize for `image-crop`.
  Pointer listeners (`pointerdown`/`pointermove`/`pointerup`/
  `pointercancel`) are wired in `connectedCallback` on `.crop-rect`
  (move) and each `.handle` (resize, one per direction in `HANDLES`).
  `pointerdown` on a handle calls `stopPropagation()` so the parent
  `.crop-rect`'s move listener doesn't also fire.
- Added pure, unit-testable coordinate/geometry functions to
  `src/crop-math.ts`: `toNaturalRect` (inverse of the existing
  `toDisplayRect`), `clampRect` (keep a rect inside bounds without
  resizing it), `moveRect` (translate + clamp), and `resizeRect`
  (grow/shrink from one of the 8 `HandleDir`s, clamped to bounds and
  a caller-supplied `minSize`). All drag math happens in *display*
  pixel space (matching pointer client-coordinate deltas 1:1); the
  result is converted to natural-image pixels via `toNaturalRect`
  only when writing back to `this.#crop` / emitting the event.
- Drag state (`#drag`) snapshots the rect (in display coords) and
  starting pointer position at `pointerdown`; every `pointermove`
  recomputes the new rect from that snapshot plus the *total* delta
  from the start (not incremental deltas), which avoids drift/
  rounding accumulation across many small move events.
- `this.emit('change', { detail: {...this.#crop} })` fires on every
  `pointermove` while dragging (not just on `pointerup`), so
  `image-crop:change` gives live natural-pixel-coordinate updates
  matching the acceptance criteria.
- `setPointerCapture` calls are wrapped in `try/catch` because
  synthetic `PointerEvent`s dispatched in the tape-run test
  environment don't correspond to a real active pointer session, and
  some environments throw `NotFoundError`/`InvalidStateError` when
  capture is requested for a pointer id with no active pointer. This
  doesn't affect real mouse/touch usage in a real browser (verified
  manually, see below).
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - `this.qsa(...)` on `WebComponent` returns a real `NodeList`
    (`this.querySelectorAll(selector)` under the hood) despite the
    published `.d.ts` typing it as returning a single element or
    null — that type declaration is wrong/misleading. Don't rely on
    `.d.ts` here; use `Array.from(this.querySelectorAll<T>(sel))`
    directly when you need to iterate, and use `this.qs<T>(sel)` with
    an explicit generic (not the default `Element`) whenever you plan
    to call type-specific methods like `addEventListener('pointerdown',
    ...)` on the result — otherwise `tsc` fails (during `npm run
    build`'s `--emitDeclarationOnly` step, NOT during `esbuild`, so
    `npm run build-tests`/tape-run passing doesn't guarantee the
    typecheck used for the final `npm test` gate also passes) with a
    "No overload matches this call" error because the inferred type is
    the generic `Element`, whose `addEventListener` overload doesn't
    accept a `(e: PointerEvent) => void` listener.
  - For drag-style pointer interactions, snapshot the *start* rect and
    pointer position on `pointerdown`, then always recompute from that
    snapshot + `(current - start)` total delta on `pointermove` — do
    not accumulate incremental per-event deltas onto the live rect, or
    floating point drift and duplicate-event edge cases compound.
  - Tested drag/resize/move/clamp/min-size/change-event behavior with
    synthetic `PointerEvent`s in `test/crop.ts` (constructing them with
    `new PointerEvent(type, { clientX, clientY, pointerId, bubbles:
    true, cancelable: true })` and reading `frame.getBoundingClientRect()`
    to anchor client coordinates to the real rendered layout) — this
    works fine in the tape-run/real-browser test harness without needing
    to mock pointer capture.
  - Verified manually in the browser with the Playwright MCP tools
    (`browser_mouse_drag_xy`) against `npm run start`, using the
    existing example page's `#crop-file` input (already wired to
    `image-crop` from the US-004 session) and a freshly generated
    400x200 test PNG: confirmed dragging an `se` corner handle resizes
    both axes, dragging the rectangle body moves it, and dragging far
    past the image bounds clamps the rect (and the `image-crop:change`
    event detail) to the maximum in-bounds position rather than
    following the pointer. Killed the vite dev server and removed
    `.playwright-mcp/` and the scratch PNG afterwards.
- Files changed: `src/crop-math.ts`, `src/crop.ts`, `test/crop-math.ts`
  (new), `test/crop.ts`, `test/index.ts` (import crop-math tests),
  `specs/prd.json` (US-005 `passes: true`).
- Tests: 8 new pure-function tests for `moveRect`/`resizeRect`/
  `toNaturalRect` in `test/crop-math.ts`, plus 4 new integration tests
  in `test/crop.ts` (corner-handle resize, minimum-size clamp,
  move-with-bounds-clamp, and the `image-crop:change` event payload).
  Full `npm test` (lint + build + typecheck + tape-run) passes with
  78 total assertions.
