## 2026-07-23 - US-007

- Added `getBlob(opts?)` and a `crop` getter to `image-crop`
  (`src/crop.ts`). `crop` returns a shallow copy of the internal
  `#crop` rect (natural-image pixels). `getBlob` draws the current
  crop region from the `<img>` element onto a freshly-created
  offscreen `<canvas>` sized to `crop.width x crop.height` via
  `ctx.drawImage(img, x, y, width, height, 0, 0, width, height)`,
  then resolves `canvas.toBlob(...)` as a Promise. `opts.type`
  defaults to `image/jpeg`; `opts.quality` is passed through
  untouched (defaults to the browser's own default when omitted, as
  `HTMLCanvasElement.toBlob` already does).
- Added `setImage(blob)` to `image-input` (`src/index.ts`) so a
  cropped Blob (e.g. from `image-crop`'s `getBlob()`) can be fed back
  in as the component's file. Widened `#file`'s type from
  `File|null` to `File|Blob|null` (and `#setFile`'s parameter type
  to match) since a Blob has no `name`/`lastModified` and there's no
  reason to require the caller to wrap it in a `File`. `setImage`
  reuses the existing `#setFile` private method (revokes the
  previous object URL, creates a new one, sets it as the preview
  `<img>` src, adds `.has-image`), then emits `image-input:change`
  with `{ file: blob, alt: this.alt ?? '' }` so consumers listening
  for `image-input:change` see the swapped-in blob the same way they
  see a freshly-picked file -- this satisfies the acceptance
  criterion that setImage "replaces ... the file emitted in
  subsequent change events".
- Object URL revocation was already handled by the existing
  `#setFile`/`#revokePreviewUrl` pattern from US-001; `setImage`
  gets this for free by routing through `#setFile` rather than
  duplicating the object-URL logic.
- Wrote failing tests first: `test/crop.ts` gained a `crop` getter
  test and two `getBlob` tests (default `image/jpeg` type + correct
  natural-resolution dimensions verified by loading the resulting
  Blob into a real `Image` and reading `naturalWidth`/`naturalHeight`;
  a second test asserting the `type` option is honored). Confirmed
  these crashed (`el.crop`/`el.getBlob` undefined) before
  implementing. `test/index.ts` gained two `setImage` tests: preview
  swap + `image-input:change` detail, and a URL-revocation test that
  monkey-patches `URL.revokeObjectURL` around the `setImage` call to
  assert the previous preview's object URL was revoked.
- Files changed: `src/crop.ts`, `src/index.ts`, `test/crop.ts`,
  `test/index.ts`, `specs/prd.json` (US-007 `passes: true`).
- Tests: 12 new tape assertions. Full `npm test` (lint + build +
  typecheck + tape-run) passes with 98 total assertions.
- No "verify in browser" step was listed in this story's acceptance
  criteria (unlike most prior stories), so none was performed; the
  canvas/Blob behavior is fully covered by the tape tests running in
  a real browser via `tape-run`.
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - To verify a Blob's actual pixel dimensions in a test without a
    dedicated image-decoding library, load it into a real `Image`
    via `URL.createObjectURL` + `img.onload`, then read
    `naturalWidth`/`naturalHeight` (see `loadBlobDimensions` in
    `test/crop.ts`) -- revoke that object URL after load to avoid
    leaking it from the test itself.
  - To assert something was cleaned up (object URL revocation, etc.)
    without a spy library, monkey-patch the global method
    (`URL.revokeObjectURL`) around just the call under test, record
    calls to an array, then restore the original -- simplest option
    given this repo has no mocking dependency.
