## 2026-07-23 - US-008

- Wired the example app (`example/index.ts`) into the full Bluesky-style
  flow: `image-input` composed with `image-crop` and an alt-text
  `<textarea>`, both hosted in native `<dialog>` elements owned by the
  example app (not the library components), demonstrating the headless
  pattern the PRD calls for -- `image-input`/`image-crop` never open a
  dialog themselves, only emit `edit`/`alt`/`change` events.
  - `image-input:edit` -> `crop.setFile(file)` + `cropDialog.showModal()`.
  - Crop dialog's Save button -> `crop.getBlob()` -> `input.setImage(blob)`
    -> `cropDialog.close()`. Cancel just closes without applying.
  - `image-input:alt` -> populate the textarea with the current alt text
    (from the event detail) -> `altDialog.showModal()`.
  - Alt dialog's Save button -> `input.alt = altTextarea.value` ->
    `altDialog.close()`.
- Removed the static `<image-input></image-input>` markup that had
  been sitting in `example/index.html` since the project scaffold --
  `example/index.ts` already builds all markup itself via
  `document.body.innerHTML +=`, so the static tag was producing a
  second, unwired `<image-input>` on the page (found while verifying
  in-browser: the page showed two "Choose File" buttons). `index.html`
  now only contains the module script tag; all markup lives in TS.
- Added `example/index.css` (new, separate from `src/index.css`) with
  minimal dialog/menu/textarea layout styling scoped to the example
  app only -- did not touch the shipped component library's CSS
  (`src/index.css`/`src/_vars.css`) since this styling isn't part of
  what consumers of the package receive.
- No dedicated automated test was added for this story: it's pure
  example-app wiring (composition of already-tested `image-input`/
  `image-crop` behavior via already-tested public APIs), and US-009 is
  the dedicated "add tests" story. A test would mostly duplicate the
  existing `image-input:edit`/`image-input:alt`/`setImage`/`getBlob`
  unit tests already covering the underlying behavior.
- Files changed: `example/index.ts`, `example/index.html`,
  `example/index.css` (new), `specs/prd.json` (US-008 `passes: true`).
- Verified manually in the browser with the Playwright MCP tools
  against `npm run start`: generated a 400x200 test PNG, uploaded it,
  clicked the pencil (edit) button and confirmed the crop dialog opens
  with the image loaded; used `Shift+ArrowLeft` on the focused crop
  rect (a pointer-drag via `browser_mouse_drag_xy` did not register as
  a real drag in this environment, same as noted for prior stories --
  keyboard interaction is not subject to that limitation and is
  equally valid for confirming the crop math is live) to shrink the
  crop to 392x200, clicked Save, and confirmed the dialog closed and
  the `image-input` preview `<img>` was replaced with a new blob URL
  whose decoded natural dimensions were exactly 392x200 (loaded via a
  fresh `Image` in `browser_evaluate`, matching the `loadBlobDimensions`
  pattern from `test/crop.ts`). Then clicked the "+ALT" badge, typed
  alt text into the dialog's textarea, clicked Save, and confirmed the
  dialog closed, the badge switched to its `has-alt`/"Edit alt text"
  state, and the preview `<img>`'s `alt` attribute was set to the
  entered text. Killed the vite dev server and removed
  `.playwright-mcp/` and the scratch PNG afterwards.
- Full `npm test` (lint + build + typecheck + tape-run) passes with 98
  total assertions (unchanged from US-007, since no new tests were
  added this story).
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - Check `example/index.html` for stray static markup left over from
    an earlier story before wiring a full composed flow in
    `example/index.ts` -- `document.body.innerHTML +=` silently
    duplicates rather than erroring if the target elements already
    exist in the static HTML, which is easy to miss without an
    in-browser check.
  - `browser_mouse_drag_xy` (Playwright MCP) does not reliably trigger
    this component's `pointerdown`/`pointermove`/`pointerup` sequence
    in this environment; prefer keyboard interaction
    (`browser_press_key` with focus set via `browser_evaluate`) to
    verify crop-rect behavior manually when a drag doesn't visibly
    register, rather than assuming the feature is broken.
