## 2026-07-24 - DT-003

- Wired `@substrate-system/drag-drop`'s `dragDrop()` onto `.box` in
  `setupEventListeners`, storing the returned cleanup function on a
  new private field `#cleanupDrop` and calling it in
  `disconnectedCallback` alongside the existing listener teardown. No
  hand-written `dragenter`/`dragover`/`dragleave` handlers were added
  -- the library itself adds/removes the `drag` class on `.box`
  (verified by reading `node_modules/@substrate-system/drag-drop/dist/
  util.js`: `addDragClass`/`removeDragClass` just toggle
  `classList`).
- `dragDrop(box, listeners)` requires a `Listener` function or
  `ListenerObject`; passed a new `handleDrop = (_record:DropRecord) =>
  {}` bound method as the `onDrop` shorthand. It's currently a no-op
  stub -- actually selecting the dropped file, syncing `input.files`,
  and emitting `image-input:change`/`:error` is DT-004/DT-005/DT-006's
  scope, deliberately left out this session per the one-task-per-
  session rule even though the task doc's own "Task 3" write-up
  bundles all of that together. Confirmed via
  `node_modules/@substrate-system/drag-drop/dist/index.js` that
  `addDragClass`/`removeDragClass` on `dragenter`/`dragleave` do not
  depend on what `onDrop` does, only on `isEventHandleable` finding a
  file-kind item/type in `dataTransfer` and `listenerObject.onDrop`
  being truthy (any non-null function satisfies that check) -- so the
  no-op stub is sufficient for this story's border-swap behavior to
  work for real, not just in the test.
- CSS: added `.box` a real border (`--image-input-border-width`
  `--image-input-border-style` `--image-input-border-color`,
  previously `.box` had no border at all -- DT-001 explicitly deferred
  it) and a `background` (`--image-input-box-bg`), plus a `.box.drag`
  override swapping to `--image-input-border-color-drag`
  (`--image-input-focus-color` reused as the value, not a new color)
  `--image-input-border-style-drag` (solid) and
  `--image-input-box-bg-drag`. Added all six as new variables in
  `src/_vars.css`. Left `.box` un-sized (no width/height/padding) and
  did not touch prompt-content styling -- those remain DT-008/DT-010's
  scope.
- Wrote a failing test first in `test/index.ts` (confirmed it failed
  with `box` never gaining the `drag` class before `dragDrop()` was
  wired up): dispatches a real `DragEvent('dragenter', ...)` with a
  `DataTransfer` that has a `File` added via `dt.items.add(file)` (so
  `isEventHandleable`'s file-kind check passes), asserts `.box` gains
  `drag`, dispatches `DragEvent('dragleave', ...)`, asserts `.box`
  loses `drag`.
- Files changed: `src/index.ts`, `src/index.css`, `src/_vars.css`,
  `test/index.ts`, `specs/drop-target.json` (DT-003 `passes: true`).
- Tests: 1 new tape test (3 new assertions). Full `npm test` (lint +
  build + typecheck + tape-run) passes with 127 total assertions.
- No "verify in browser" step was listed in DT-003's acceptance
  criteria, so none was performed.
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - `dragDrop()`'s dragenter/dragleave class toggling is entirely
    independent of the `onDrop` callback's implementation -- a no-op
    `onDrop` is enough to get the drag-class CSS working for real. Use
    this to land the library wiring and CSS in one story (DT-003)
    ahead of the actual drop-handling logic (DT-004+) without
    faking/skipping anything.
  - For synthetic `DragEvent` tests against this library, add a real
    `File` to the test `DataTransfer` via `dt.items.add(file)` --
    `isEventHandleable` in the library requires a file-kind item (or a
    `"Files"` type) present, or it silently no-ops and the class never
    gets added.
